[{:db/id -1, :doc/id "doc-ayatori-bench-coscientist-iteration-35", :doc/title "ayatori Co-Scientist Iteration 35 — the grouped ordered drive: IC02 on the full SF1 82 → 0.4 ms; sf1-full 1731 / 1985 ops/s", :doc/path "bench/coscientist-iteration-35.edn", :doc/body "# Co-Scientist iteration 35 - the date-ordered reads at full scale: merge the bound groups, not the whole value order\n\n> Seed: iteration 34's S25 -- IC02 141 ms / IC09 113 / IS2 28 ms p50 on\n> the full SF1. Judge: bench/benchmark.edn v2; rung :sf1-full; SUT levi\n> -Xmx10g, driver judah, LAN; impl :datalog.\n\n## Generate\n\n  Measured first (kotobase-peer bench/ic02_scale_diag, in one process on\n  the full SF1, 40 official IC02 parameters, engine / plain join / twin /\n  raw index stages interleaved):\n\n    engine 82.4 ms   plain 78.6   twin 20.3   raw 19.4 (dates 12.1, top 6.4)\n    friends 11       candidates 21,417        in range 16,954\n\n  The drive's wasted 512-value walk was 4 ms. The plain join paid 3.8 us\n  per candidate against 0.9 for the hand-written path -- and even that\n  path touched every one of 21k candidates. The shape was the wall.\n\n  Hypothesis: when the driver's entity is tied through `[?e g ?f]` to a\n  `?f` the independent half binds (messages by each FRIEND), merge the\n  g-groups of those ?f values in value order and read at most `limit`\n  entries from each. `datalog.index/grouped-values` holds per pair [g a]\n  the sorted-set of `[v e]` under each gv, built on first use and carried\n  across writes like the value order; the drive k-way merges the groups'\n  windows; chunks end on value boundaries so ties never straddle two.\n\n  Prediction, written first: IC02 in process falls from ~80 ms to a few\n  ms; served IC02/IC09/IS2 fall likewise; IC07/IC08 do not move (their\n  queries carry no :order-by/:limit -- the harness sorts after the join);\n  the 8-slot read ceiling rises several-fold.\n\n## Rank\n\n    in process, same 40 parameters:   82.4 -> 0.44 ms   (185x, 0 disagreements)\n\n    served p50 (ms)   ic02   ic09   is2   is3   ic07   ic08\n      iteration 34    141    113    28     8     31    0.5\n      iteration 35    0.2    9.2    0.1   4.4   16.8   0.2\n\n    rung sf1-full  slots  reads/s  tcr      upd/s  on-time%  thru/s\n      it.34           8     128    0.0002   1092    100.00   1215.8\n      it.35           8     640    0.0002   1092    100.00   1730.8   (768: 92.9% fails)\n      it.35          32     512    0.00015  1475    100.00   1984.8   (640: 85.9% fails)\n\n  A hazard found on the way and closed: with 16 tracked order versions,\n  at 1,000 inserts/s a reader whose independent half outlived sixteen\n  writes found its snapshot untracked and rebuilt the 3M-entry group\n  order -- ~20 s, seen as IS2/IC09 max 20-23 s in the first sweep.\n  Versions 16 -> 1024 (a version is a path copy); /ldbc/meta now reports\n  the rebuild counters: 1 build, 0 rebuilds across 205,344 inserts.\n\n  Tests: datalog JVM 134/416/0, cljs 138/427/0 (equivalence with the plain\n  join on every LDBC shape including ties across chunks and IS2's single\n  group; carry across assert and retract with zero rebuilds; a control\n  without a bound group plans no group). kotobase-peer 262/804/0.\n\n## Reflect\n\n  - Prediction held on all four parts. What made it cheap to be right was\n    the diagnostic: the before-numbers said 'shape', so the fix was a\n    merge, not another engine constant.\n  - The remaining wall on this rung is IC07 (16.8 ms) and IC08 -- both\n    sort in the harness after a full join. Writing their top-20 into the\n    query lets the grouped drive take them (IC07: one row per liker needs\n    a fetch-more loop because a liker can hold several of the top likes).\n  - Variance: an 8-slot sweep right after a restart failed at 256 reads\n    and a later one passed 640. The first minutes after a restart carry\n    JIT and first-use builds; rows are taken after a warm run and every\n    column ends on a failing rate (iteration 33's rule).\n\n## Meta\n\nVerdict under bench/benchmark.edn v2: GROWTH (same rung, same mix, 8-slot\nread ceiling 128 -> 640; first passing 32-slot row on the full SF1).\nRatio to the audited No.1: 70 at 32 slots.\n\nSeeds for iteration 36, in order:\n\n  - S27: IC07/IC08 top-k written into the query (grouped drive on\n    like/message and replyOf).\n  - S26: a full-data parameter set.\n  - S23b: IC05 so INS5 has a covering read.\n  - M3: SF3 (~13 GB) -- persisted plane or a larger host.\n"}]
